.he "time (1)                     10/3/26                     time (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
time
- time a command
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
time command [ args ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Time runs command with the given arguments and, when it finishes,
reports how long it took.  The report is written on the standard error
as
.sp
.ce
.bd
real NmSs
.ce
user N sys N
.sp
where real is the wall clock in minutes and seconds from time(2), and
user and sys are the cycle counts that times(2) returns, differenced
across the child's run.  The simulator has no separate system-time
clock, so sys is always 0.  On a kernel whose times(2) is still unimp
the user and sys lines are omitted.  Command is looked up in ., /bin
and /usr/bin, so time cc hello.c works without a path.
.sp
Time's own exit status is the command's, or 1 if the command could not
be found or forked.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
date (1), times (2)
